---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_ACRONYM, SITE_EXPANDED, SITE_GITHUB_URL, SITE_LICENSE_URL } from '../consts';
import { computeGlobalClaimStats } from '../lib/claims';
import { computeLedgerStats, getAllCitations } from '../lib/ledgers';

const cases = await getCollection('cases');
const citationStats = computeLedgerStats(getAllCitations());
const claimStats = computeGlobalClaimStats();
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Evidence Standards, Claim Architecture & Research Protocols | ${SITE_ACRONYM}`}
  description="How DRAgNEt structures its three-layer architecture (Case Files, Claims, Citations), separates Documentary Findings from Interpretive Hypotheses, distinguishes AI evaluation from human review, and enforces archival rights."
>
  <article class="split-section" style="padding: 2.4rem 0 3rem;">
    <header class="page-intro">
    <div style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em;">
      Methodology &amp; Governance
    </div>
    <h1 style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.85rem, 3.2vw, 2.45rem); margin: 0.35rem 0 0.85rem;">
      Evidence Standards, Claim Architecture &amp; Protocols
    </h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.06rem; color: var(--ink-muted);">
      Readers have good reason to be skeptical of AI-assisted historical research: automated searches routinely mistake broken
      newsprint type for early words, trust misdated bound-volume catalog records, or overstate speculative etymologies.
      <strong> {SITE_ACRONYM}</strong> ({SITE_EXPANDED}) is built on a simple principle: rather than asking anyone to trust an
      agent, decompose every investigation into primary scans, atomic citation records, and bounded, falsifiable claims so
      trusting the agent is unnecessary.
    </p>

    </header>
    <div class="reading-spread">
      <nav class="reading-rail" aria-label="Evidence and methods sections">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#three-layer-architecture">Three-layer architecture</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-governance">Claim assessments &amp; boundaries</a></li>
          <li><a href="#record-classification">Citation types &amp; verification</a></li>
          <li><a href="#automated-checks">What automation checks</a></li>
          <li><a href="#research-system">Research system</a></li>
          <li><a href="#publication-policy">Publication policy</a></li>
          <li><a href="#rights">Licensing and image rights</a></li>
          <li><a href="#corrections">Corrections log</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
      <div class="reading-column">
    <h2 id="three-layer-architecture">1. Three-Layer Evidence Architecture (Case Files, Claims, Citations)</h2>
    <p>
      To prevent narrative monographs from blurring documentary facts with linguistic reconstruction, {SITE_ACRONYM} organizes
      its corpus across three bidirectionally linked layers—all computed dynamically at build time from version-controlled
      files:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Layer 1 — <a href="/">Case Files</a> ({cases.length} published monographs in <code>src/content/cases/*.md</code>):</strong>{' '}
        Human-readable philological studies synthesizing prior scholarship, historical context, attestation timelines, proof
        plates, rejected leads, and open archival gaps for each target headword.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Layer 2 — <a href="/claims/">Structured Claims</a> ({claimStats.activeClaims} active propositions across {claimStats.claimLedgersCount} claim ledgers in <code>public/claims/*.json</code>):</strong>{' '}
        Discrete, individually addressable propositions linking Case File arguments to underlying <code>CERT-*</code> witnesses
        and upstream <code>CLM-*</code> premises, while generating automatic reverse backlinks on every citation page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Layer 3 — <a href="/citations/">Primary Citations &amp; Scans</a> ({citationStats.totalEvaluated} evaluated records and {citationStats.hostedScans} hosted pre-1931 scans in <code>public/ledgers/*.json</code>):</strong>{' '}
        Atomic primary-source attestations, comparative controls, evaluated donor hypotheses, and exposed OCR or misdated-serial
        ghosts with verbatim diplomatic transcriptions and direct host-archive links.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="claim-governance">2. Documentary Findings vs. Interpretive Hypotheses &amp; Review Honesty</h2>
    <p>
      Every record in <a href="/claims/">the Claim Index</a> (such as <a href="/claims/gimmick.json"><code>public/claims/gimmick.json</code></a> and{' '}
      <a href="/claims/shill-shillaber.json"><code>public/claims/shill-shillaber.json</code></a>) enforces four anti-overclaiming
      rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Documentary Findings vs. Interpretive Hypotheses:</strong> Claims of type <code>attestation</code>,{' '}
        <code>antedating</code>, <code>negative_evidence</code>, and <code>source_relationship</code> are classified as{' '}
        <strong>Documentary Findings</strong> ({claimStats.documentaryClaims} currently active). Claims of type{' '}
        <code>semantic</code>, <code>historical_context</code>, <code>transmission</code>, <code>derivation</code>,{' '}
        <code>phonological</code>, and <code>rebuttal</code> are classified as <strong>Interpretive Hypotheses</strong> (
        {claimStats.interpretiveClaims} currently active).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Five-State Evidentiary Assessment without Auto-Inheritance:</strong> Each proposition is graded{' '}
        <code>supported</code>, <code>partially_supported</code>, <code>inconclusive</code>, <code>contradicted</code>, or{' '}
        <code>unassessed</code>, with zero numerical confidence percentages and zero green checkmarks. An interpretive
        hypothesis never automatically inherits <code>supported</code> status merely because its documentary{' '}
        <code>depends_on</code> premises are supported.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mandatory Three-Part Evidence Boundary:</strong> Every claim specifies in plain prose (1){' '}
        <em>what the evidence establishes</em>, (2) <em>what it does not establish</em>, and (3){' '}
        <em>remaining uncertainty</em>—paired with explicit falsification/resolution criteria on unresolved interpretive
        hypotheses, date-precision guardrails on antedatings, and search manifests on bounded negative evidence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unmistakable AI-Agent vs. Human Review Status &amp; Stale-Review Fingerprinting:</strong> Review status (
        <code>unreviewed</code>, <code>agent_reviewed</code>, <code>independently_agent_reviewed</code>,{' '}
        <code>human_reviewed</code>) is tracked orthogonally from evidentiary assessment. Neither <code>agent_reviewed</code>{' '}
        nor <code>independently_agent_reviewed</code> represents human peer review; <code>human_reviewed</code> is blocked in
        CI unless a named human scholar and non-stale upholding review record are present. Every review records SHA-256
        material fingerprints of the claim and its cited evidence so subsequent edits automatically flag prior reviews as stale.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="record-classification">3. Separating What a Citation Record Is from Who Verified It</h2>
    <p>
      Every primary record in our citation ledgers (<a href="/ledgers/shill-shillaber.json"><code>shill-shillaber.json</code></a>,{' '}
      <a href="/ledgers/gimmick.json"><code>gimmick.json</code></a>) carries two independent classifications:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Record Type (<code>record_type</code>):</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><code>attestation</code> — A dated primary-source occurrence of the headword or variant in historical print.</li>
          <li><code>contextual_evidence</code> — A comparative catalog, patent, prosopographical record, or negative-corpus control.</li>
          <li><code>hypothesis</code> — A linguistic donor, pronunciation, or morphological comparison (marked either <em>supporting</em> or <em>weakened/unlikely</em>—scholarly proposals weakened by new evidence are never labeled "myths").</li>
          <li><code>rejected_lead</code> — A demonstrable false lead: an optical character recognition (OCR) misread, a misdated bound serial, an unrelated proper surname, or an uncorroborated folk-etymological claim.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verification Level (<code>verification_level</code>):</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Discovered (<code>discovered</code>):</strong> Candidate citation surfaced via archival search or secondary literature; original page scan not yet inspected.</li>
          <li><strong>Agent-verified (<code>agent_verified</code>):</strong> An AI research agent inspected the page image, checked the printed issue masthead or edition date against catalog metadata, and transcribed the passage. Records stay at <code>agent_verified</code> until a named human independently inspects them.</li>
          <li><strong>Human-verified (<code>human_verified</code>):</strong> A named human reviewer (recorded in <code>human_verified_by</code> and <code>human_verified_date</code>) independently inspected the original scan and verified the date, transcription, and sense.</li>
          <li><strong>Externally recognized (<code>externally_recognized</code>):</strong> Citation or finding already recorded in or subsequently accepted by a historical dictionary (<em>OED</em>, <em>Green's Dictionary of Slang</em>, <em>HDAS</em>), peer-reviewed journal, or independent specialist.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="automated-checks">4. What Our Automated Validation Scripts Actually Check (and What They Cannot Check)</h2>
    <p>
      On every build and Pull Request,{' '}
      <a href={`${SITE_GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/scripts/validate_submission.py`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>scripts/validate_submission.py</code></a>,{' '}
      <code>scripts/validate-site.mjs</code>, and <code>scripts/validate-claims.mjs</code> / <code>scripts/validate_claims.py</code>{' '}
      run before Astro builds the site. To avoid overstating automated checks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Verified by software:</strong> Citation and claim JSON schema validity; bidirectional cross-referencing of every <code>CERT-*</code> and <code>CLM-*</code> identifier; acyclicity (DAG) of inferential claim dependencies; date-precision arithmetic on antedating baselines; deterministic SHA-256 content fingerprinting and stale-review detection; presence and size limits of hosted scans; HTTPS archive URLs; exclusion of raw similarity scores, confidence percentages, or decimal-year antedatings; blocking <code>human_verified</code> / <code>human_reviewed</code> status without a named human verifier; blocking local hosting of post-1930 scans; and stripping internal workspace or cloud-drive identifiers.</li>
      <li><strong>Requires human philological review:</strong> Diplomatic accuracy of transcriptions against worn ink, semantic interpretation in context, completeness of prior-scholarship credit, and substantive soundness of linguistic arguments.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="research-system">5. Reproducible Research System &amp; Corpus Manifests</h2>
    <p>
      The repository includes the underlying research protocols, schemas, and search utilities in{' '}
      <a href={`${SITE_GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/research-system`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>research-system/</code></a>{' '}
      and <code>schemas/</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>research-system/01_research_and_verification_protocol.md</code> — Archival search protocol, printed-masthead dating checks, OCR-error screening, and editorial calibration rules.</li>
      <li><code>research-system/ocr_phonetic_harness.py</code> — Zero-dependency Python 3 utility for generating 18th/19th-century broken-type OCR search variants, consonant-skeleton triage scores, and Chronicling America / Internet Archive query URLs.</li>
      <li><code>schemas/claim-ledger.schema.json</code> &amp; <code>docs/claim-architecture.md</code> — Formal JSON Schema (Draft 2020-12) and specification for claim-level evidence graphs.</li>
      <li><code>public/ledgers/*.json</code> &amp; <code>public/claims/*.json</code> — Machine-readable citation ledgers, corpus search manifests, multi-edition dictionary tables, and structured claim graphs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="publication-policy">6. Academic Journal Prior-Dissemination Policy</h2>
    <p>
      Because lexicographical findings may also be submitted to peer-reviewed journals (such as <em>American Speech</em>,{' '}
      <em>Notes and Queries</em>, or <em>Comments on Etymology</em>), every investigation tracks an explicit{' '}
      <code>publicationStatus</code> (<code>working_paper</code>, <code>manuscript_in_preparation</code>, <code>under_review</code>,{' '}
      <code>published_article</code>, or <code>dictionary_submitted</code>). While publishers such as Oxford University Press permit
      posting an unrefereed Author's Original Version (preprint) prior to submission, other journals restrict overlapping prose
      dissemination. Check the target journal's current preprint and self-archiving rules before publishing overlapping manuscript
      text on {SITE_ACRONYM}, and update the Case File with the Version of Record DOI upon publication.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights">7. Licensing &amp; Third-Party Archival Image Rights</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Original Research Prose &amp; Structured Metadata:</strong> Licensed under <a href={SITE_LICENSE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Website Source Code &amp; Scripts:</strong> Licensed under the MIT License.</li>
      <li><strong>Archival Page Scans:</strong> Separated strictly from our CC BY 4.0 license. Only works published in the United States on or before <strong>December 31, 1930</strong> (in the U.S. public domain as of January 1, 2026) are hosted directly in <code>public/scans/</code>. Works published in <strong>1931 or later</strong> (such as Walter A. Schwartz's 1931 <em>CIGAM</em> and 1933 <em>GIMAC</em> booklets held at State Library Victoria) are never redistributed in the repository and are linked directly at their host archive viewer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="corrections">8. Versioned Corrections &amp; Retractions Log</h2>
    <p>
      Substantive factual corrections, count reconciliations, and reclassified citations are logged permanently in{' '}
      <a href={`${SITE_GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/CHANGELOG_CORRECTIONS.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>CHANGELOG_CORRECTIONS.md</code></a>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2026-11-25 (v3.0 Audit — <code>gimmick</code> 1893 Baseline Paraphrase Fix):</strong> Corrected three unverified paraphrase glosses in the preliminary <code>prior_art_calibration</code> metadata of Case 1100 (which had erroneously glossed OED3's 24 Feb. 1893 <em>Bangor Daily News</em> quotation as referring to pool rooms and 1906 <em>Connecticut Western News</em> as "hand-shaking gimmick"). Verified directly against <em>OED Online</em> (<code>gimmick, n.</code>) that OED3's 24 Feb. 1893 quotation is indeed the Bucksport electric-plant engine item (<em>"caused the flopper to fall down on the mizzen reciprocal eccentricity gimmick"</em>), matching the 11 March 1893 <em>Electrical Review</em> scan (<code>CERT-900-101</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>2026-11-25 (v3.0 Audit — Single-Source Ledger Counts &amp; Reclassification):</strong> Removed hardcoded frontmatter counts (which had drifted to 20/43 vs. 25/38 in Case 1100) so all counts are computed dynamically at build time from <code>public/ledgers/*.json</code>; separated 27 comparative catalog, prosopographical, and negative-corpus controls (<code>contextual_evidence</code>) and 11 weakened donor comparisons (<code>hypothesis</code>) from genuine OCR/misdate <code>rejected_lead</code> records.</li>
      <li><strong>2026-11-25 (v3.0 Audit — Post-1930 Image Removal &amp; Workspace Sanitization):</strong> Removed locally bundled 1931/1933 booklet images from repository storage in favor of direct State Library Victoria viewer links, hosted all 66 pre-1931 U.S. public-domain scans, and stripped internal cloud-storage identifiers from all public JSON files.</li>
    </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
